WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.800
The ship sank. Who pays for the loss? The answer is in three letters on your invoice.

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:10.135
They look like this: CIF Santos. The code is the rule, the place is where it applies.

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:15.744
Every import follows this path. The Incoterm says where risk passes to the buyer.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:18.126
Group E, minimum obligation.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:21.611
EXW, the buyer picks up and takes on everything.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:23.780
Group F, the main freight is on the buyer.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:27.400
FCA, delivery to the buyer's carrier.

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:29.985
FAS, the cargo is left alongside the ship.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:33.386
FOB, risk passes once the cargo is on board.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:37.700
FOB Shanghai. The cargo falls off the crane before loading. Who pays?

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.800
The seller. It was not on board yet.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:42.322
Group C, the seller pays the freight, not the risk.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:45.233
CPT, carriage paid to destination.

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:47.822
CIP, the same. With broad insurance.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.344
CFR, freight to the port, risk on board.

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:53.779
CIF, the same. With minimum insurance.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:58.336
CIF Santos. The ship sinks at sea. Who bears the loss?

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.688
The buyer. They claim the insurance.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:03.181
Group D, the seller takes it to destination.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:05.961
DAP, delivered at destination, not unloaded.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:08.487
DPU, the seller also unloads.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:12.108
DDP, the seller even pays the import duties.

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:19.499
Insurance is only mandatory in CIP and CIF. Always write the rule and the place. Understand the process and import better.
